  <section class="btc-sec" id="btc-what">
    <h2>What Is a Body Type Calculator and How Does It Work?</h2>
    <p>A body type calculator is a measurement-based tool that compares your bust, waist and hip circumferences to classify your figure into one of several recognised shapes. It calculates the ratios and differences between your measurements and matches them against established formulas — the same ones used in clothing science research. Unlike a weighing scale or BMI chart, it does not measure how much you weigh; it tells you how your measurements relate to each other, which determines your overall silhouette.</p>
    <p>The logic is straightforward: bust and hip measurements are compared to see which is larger and by how much, then the waist is compared to both to determine how defined it is. Based on those differences, the calculator places you into one of seven body shapes — hourglass, top hourglass, bottom hourglass, pear, apple, inverted triangle or rectangle.</p>
    <div class="btc-note">
      <p><strong>This is a fashion and self-awareness tool, not a health screening device.</strong> The result is a starting point for understanding your proportions and finding styles that suit you — not a medical classification or a value judgment.</p>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Seven shapes beat four.</strong> Older calculators use only four. This one uses a seven-shape system based on research published in the <em>International Journal of Clothing Science and Technology</em>, which gives a more precise result and reduces the "I fall between two shapes" problem.</li>
      <li><strong>Accuracy depends on your measurements.</strong> Measure carefully and the result is genuinely useful. Rush, and the calculator is working with bad data.</li>
      <li><strong>You get more than a label.</strong> Every result comes with outfit direction and a waist-to-hip ratio reading.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="btc-sec" id="btc-measure">
    <h2>How to Take Accurate Body Measurements at Home</h2>
    <p>Accurate measurements equal accurate results. Grab a flexible measuring tape (the soft fabric kind used for sewing, not a metal builder's tape), stand in front of a mirror if you can, and breathe normally throughout. Stand straight with your arms relaxed and feet together. This calculator works equally well in centimeters or inches — use the toggle above and enter every measurement in the same unit.</p>

    <h3>How to Measure Your Bust Size</h3>
    <p>Wrap the tape around the fullest part of your chest, usually across the nipple line. If you are measuring in a bra, wear a properly fitted, non-padded one. The tape should sit level across your back and front — don't let it dip or ride up at the back. Keep it snug against the skin without compressing anything, breathe naturally, then take the reading.</p>

    <h3>How to Measure Your Waist Size</h3>
    <p>Your natural waist is the narrowest part of your torso, usually an inch or two <em>above</em> the belly button rather than at it — that mix-up is the most common measuring mistake. Don't suck in your stomach; exhale gently and measure your relaxed shape, keeping the tape parallel to the floor. Not sure where your natural waist is? Bend sideways — the crease that forms is the spot.</p>

    <h3>How to Measure Your Hip Size</h3>
    <p>Stand with your feet together and wrap the tape around the widest part of your buttocks and hips, usually about 7–9 inches below the natural waist. Keep the tape level all the way around and check the back in a mirror so it doesn't tilt downward. Measure over bare skin or form-fitting clothes, never over thick fabric.</p>

    <h3>What Is High Hip and How Do You Measure It?</h3>
    <p>The high hip is measured roughly 3 to 4 inches (8–10 cm) below your natural waist, across the top of the pelvic bone — the widest point of the upper hip area, distinct from the full hip below. Most calculators skip it entirely. This tool uses it to show your High Hip–Waist ratio alongside your other proportions, which is useful supplementary context even though your bust, waist and hip numbers alone determine your shape category.</p>

    <h3>Inches vs. Centimeters — Which Should You Use?</h3>
    <p>Both work equally well; the calculator accepts either unit. What matters is consistency — measure everything in the same unit. Mixing inches and centimeters will give you an incorrect result. If you have a tape that only shows one unit and want the other, multiply centimeters by 0.394 to get inches, or inches by 2.54 to get centimeters. Most modern measuring tapes show both units on opposite sides.</p>

    <div class="btc-note">
      <p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ask a friend to take your measurements if possible — self-measuring, especially at the back, can introduce errors. Measure in the morning before meals for the most consistent results. Want the complete rundown — chest, thigh, inseam and more? Our full <a href="/body-measurements/">body measurements</a> guide covers every point of the tape.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="btc-sec" id="btc-shapes">
    <h2>The 7 Female Body Shapes Explained (With Formulas)</h2>
    <p>Most people know the classic four: hourglass, pear, apple and rectangle. Real bodies are more nuanced. A seven-shape system — supported by research from North Carolina State University and used in clothing science literature — captures those nuances far more accurately, including two shapes older four-category systems collapse into "pear": bottom hourglass and top hourglass. The percentages below are the most widely cited figures, drawn from a 2006 analysis of over 6,300 women; later re-analyses using different methods have found different splits, so treat the numbers as a general picture rather than an exact census. Every shape has its own styling strengths; none is more "correct" than another.</p>

    <div class="btc-tbl-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Shape</th><th scope="col">Roughly how common</th><th scope="col">Defining formula (inches)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Rectangle / Banana</td><td>~46%</td><td>Bust &amp; hips within 3.6 in; waist less than 9 in smaller</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pear / Triangle</td><td>~20%</td><td>Hips ≥ 3.6 in wider than bust; hips − waist &lt; 9 in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Apple / Oval</td><td>~14%</td><td>Bust &amp; hips within 3.6 in of each other; waist within 2 in of — or larger than — both bust and hips</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hourglass</td><td>~8%</td><td>Bust − hips ≤ 1 in and hips − bust &lt; 3.6 in; waist ≥ 9 in smaller than bust (or ≥ 10 in smaller than hips)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Top Hourglass</td><td>Less common</td><td>Bust 1–10 in larger than hips; bust − waist ≥ 9 in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bottom Hourglass</td><td>Less common</td><td>Hips 3.6–10 in larger than bust; hips − waist ≥ 9 in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inverted Triangle</td><td>Less common</td><td>Bust ≥ 3.6 in wider than hips; bust − waist &lt; 9 in</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 id="hourglass-body-shape">Hourglass Body Shape</h3>
    <p>The hourglass has a bust and hips that are roughly equal, with a waist significantly narrower than both — the bust is no more than 1 inch bigger than the hips (and the hips no more than 3.6 inches bigger than the bust), and the waist is at least 9 inches smaller than the bust or 10 inches smaller than the hips. Only about 8% of women have a true hourglass figure, making it far rarer than most people assume. If this is your result, your proportions are already balanced — the main styling rule is to keep them visible rather than hiding them under boxy shapes. See our full <a href="/hourglass-body-shape/">hourglass body shape</a> guide for the deeper breakdown, including how it compares to top and bottom hourglass.</p>
    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-hourglass-3d-profile.webp" width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D hourglass body shape, front and three-quarter view, with 39 in bust, 27 in waist and 40 in hips traced by an hourglass outline">
    </figure>


    <h3 id="top-hourglass-body-shape">Top Hourglass Body Shape <span class="btc-unique">Distinct from pear</span></h3>
    <p>Similar to the classic hourglass, but the bust is larger than the hips, typically by 1 to 10 inches. The waist is still clearly defined (at least 9 inches smaller than the bust), so the silhouette keeps its pinched look while the balance tilts upward. This distinction matters for necklines and top choices — if generic "hourglass tips" have never quite worked for you, this may be why. Draw attention downward with patterned or wide-leg trousers, A-line skirts in bold colors, and V-necklines that lengthen rather than broaden the upper body.</p>
    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-top-hourglass-3d-profile.webp" width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D top hourglass body shape with 42 in bust, 30 in waist and 37 in hips, traced by an hourglass outline that is wider at the top">
    </figure>

    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-top-hourglass-3d-styling.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D styling comparison for a top hourglass shape — avoid: high neck with a tight pencil skirt; choose: V-neck top with an A-line skirt">
    </figure>


    <h3 id="bottom-hourglass-body-shape">Bottom Hourglass Body Shape <span class="btc-unique">Distinct from pear</span></h3>
    <p>The mirror image of the top hourglass: hips are 3.6 to 10 inches larger than the bust, but the waist stays clearly defined, at least 9 inches smaller than the hips. Four-shape systems usually collapse this into "pear" — the real difference is waist definition: pear shapes have a softer waist, bottom hourglass shapes have a strongly cinched one. Balance your proportions by adding volume to the upper body — boat necklines, puff sleeves and structured blazers work beautifully, paired with A-line or fit-and-flare skirts.</p>
    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-bottom-hourglass-3d-profile.webp" width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D bottom hourglass body shape with 34 in bust, 28 in waist and 42 in hips, traced by an hourglass outline that is wider at the bottom">
    </figure>

    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-bottom-hourglass-3d-styling.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D styling comparison for a bottom hourglass shape — avoid: tight bodycon dress; choose: boat-neck fit-and-flare dress with puff sleeves">
    </figure>


    <h3 id="pear-triangle-body-shape">Pear (Triangle) Body Shape</h3>
    <p>Also called the triangle or bell shape, sometimes searched as "glute shape" or "butt shape." Hips are noticeably wider than the bust, typically by 3.6 inches or more, with a waist that is present but not dramatically defined (less than 9 inches smaller than the hips). Fullness concentrates in the hips, thighs and bottom. <strong>Best outfits:</strong> A-line skirts, bootcut jeans, wrap tops, off-shoulder necklines and bold prints on top. <strong>Avoid:</strong> very tight skirts that add bulk below, and pale or nude bottoms that draw attention to the hips. Our full <a href="/pear-body-shape/">pear body shape</a> guide covers formal, casual and swimwear picks in more depth.</p>
    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-pear-3d-profile.webp" width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D pear body shape with 36 in bust, 35 in waist and 43 in hips, traced by a pear outline">
    </figure>


    <h3>Is Pear the Same as "Spoon" Body Shape?</h3>
    <p>Some calculators list "spoon" as its own category, distinct from pear. The pattern they're describing is a pear shape with a pronounced high-hip shelf — where the high hip (measured about 3 to 4 inches below the waist) is significantly larger than the waist itself, rather than tapering gradually into the hip. In practice this is a variant of pear rather than a separate silhouette: the styling advice is the same (draw attention upward, balance with A-line or straight-leg bottoms), but a wrap top or peplum that sits right at the high-hip line can look more flattering than one that ends higher up, since it works with the shelf instead of cutting across it. If your result above mentions a "shelf pattern," this is what it's referring to.</p>

    <h3 id="apple-body-shape">Apple Body Shape</h3>
    <p>The apple shape carries fullness through the middle: bust and hips stay fairly close in size, but the waist doesn't taper much between them, with weight often sitting around the belly, chest and upper back. <strong>Apple isn't a weight category</strong> — a slim person can be apple-shaped and a larger person can be an hourglass; it's about proportion, not size. Create the illusion of a waist with wrap dresses, empire-waist styles and V-necks. Avoid wide belts cinched at the natural waist — they sit on the fullest point rather than defining it. See our full <a href="/apple-body-shape/">apple body shape</a> guide for the exact formula and how it differs from rectangle.</p>
    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-apple-3d-profile.webp" width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D apple body shape with 40 in bust, 41 in waist and 41 in hips, traced by an apple outline">
    </figure>


    <h3 id="inverted-triangle-body-shape">Inverted Triangle Body Shape</h3>
    <p>The opposite of the pear: shoulders and bust are more than 3.6 inches wider than the hips, and the waist-to-bust difference is under 9 inches. Common among naturally broad-shouldered women and those who train the upper body heavily — swimmers often develop this silhouette. Add visual volume below the waist with A-line and flared skirts, wide-leg trousers and bootcut jeans; deep V-necks work better than boat necks, which emphasise shoulder width further. Our full <a href="/inverted-triangle-body-shape/">inverted triangle body shape</a> guide covers a waist-to-hip ratio quirk specific to this shape.</p>
    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-inverted-triangle-3d-profile.webp" width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D inverted triangle body shape with 41 in bust, 33 in waist and 35 in hips, traced by a downward-pointing triangle">
    </figure>


    <h3 id="rectangle-banana-straight-body-shape">Rectangle (Banana/Straight) Body Shape</h3>
    <p>The most common shape, roughly 46% of women according to NC State research. Bust, waist and hips are all similar — less than 3.6 inches between bust and hips, and less than 9 inches between the waist and either. Create the curves and definition the proportions don't naturally provide: peplum tops, ruffled skirts, belted styles and layering all work well, and bootcut or flared jeans add width at the hem for a more balanced proportion.</p>
    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-rectangle-3d-profile.webp" width="1200" height="820" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D rectangle body shape with 35 in bust, 31 in waist and 36 in hips, traced by a straight rectangle outline">
    </figure>

    <figure class="btc-fig">
      <img src="/assets/images/3d-guide/female-rectangle-3d-styling.webp" width="1200" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="3D styling comparison for a rectangle shape — avoid: straight shift dress; choose: belted shirt dress">
    </figure>

  </section>

