  <section class="btc-sec" id="btc-heightweight">
    <h2>Looking for a Body Shape Calculator by Height and Weight?</h2>
    <p>If you searched for a body shape calculator using height and weight, it's worth knowing upfront: hourglass, pear, apple and the other classic shapes are defined by the relationship between bust, waist and hip measurements, not by overall size — two people with identical height and weight can have completely different shapes. Height and weight alone can tell you your BMI and, with your wrist added, your body frame size, but not your shape category. If that's specifically what you're after, our <a href="/body-frame-size-calculator/">body frame size calculator</a> covers that directly. For an actual visual — a 3D figure you can rotate, scaled to your height and weight — try our <a href="/body-visualizer/">body visualizer</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="btc-sec" id="btc-health">
    <h2>Waist-to-Hip Ratio, Waist-to-Height Ratio, and What Your Shape Says About Health</h2>
    <p>Body shape and health are connected, but not the way most people assume. Your shape is not a diagnosis. A pear shape is not automatically healthier than an apple shape, and an hourglass figure doesn't signal excellent health. What the research does tell us is that <strong>where</strong> your body stores fat is a meaningful signal — and that's exactly what waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) is built to capture. For all your ratios in one place — waist-to-hip, waist-to-height, chest-to-waist and more — use our <a href="/body-proportions-calculator/">body proportions calculator</a>.</p>

    <h3>What Is Waist-to-Hip Ratio and Why Does It Matter?</h3>
    <p>WHR is your waist measurement divided by your hip measurement — a 30-inch waist and 40-inch hips gives a WHR of 0.75. It's a proxy for whether more of your fat sits around the abdomen (central fat) versus the hips and thighs (peripheral fat) — a circumference-based estimate, not a direct measurement of fat itself, but a distinction that matters for health-risk assessment.</p>
    <div class="btc-tbl-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Sex</th><th scope="col">WHO Threshold</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Women</td><td>Above 0.85 is classified as abdominal obesity</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Men</td><td>Above 0.90 is classified as abdominal obesity</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>These are the WHO's published cutoffs, not a graded scale — there's no separate "moderate" tier below them from the WHO itself. The INTERHEART study, led by Salim Yusuf and colleagues and published in <em>The Lancet</em> in 2004, tracked heart attack risk across more than 50 countries and found WHR outperformed BMI as a predictor of myocardial infarction, largely because BMI can't distinguish someone carrying abdominal fat from someone carrying the same weight elsewhere. A 2015 study of over 15,000 adults, published in the <em>Annals of Internal Medicine</em>, went further: people with a <em>normal</em> BMI but a high WHR ("normal-weight central obesity") had worse long-term survival than people classified as obese by BMI alone. Get the fuller breakdown on our dedicated <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist-to-hip ratio calculator</a>.</p>

    <h3>Waist-to-Height Ratio — A Second Number Worth Checking</h3>
    <p>Waist-to-height ratio (WHtR) divides your waist measurement by your height, using the same unit for both. A ratio below 0.5 — keeping your waist under half your height — is the widely cited healthy target, and several studies suggest it flags cardiometabolic risk earlier than BMI does, since it doesn't depend on frame size. Because it only needs waist and height, it's a useful second check alongside WHR — and it's also part of current 2026 U.S. military body-composition standards. Our <a href="/waist-to-height-ratio-calculator/">waist to height ratio calculator</a> walks through it in full, including the military standards.</p>

    <h3>Apple vs. Pear — Fat Distribution and Cardiovascular Risk</h3>
    <p>Research published by the WHO and in journals including <em>The Lancet</em> has linked central obesity — the pattern the apple body shape tends to reflect — with elevated risk for cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, hypertension and metabolic syndrome. Abdominal fat tends to include a higher share of visceral fat, which surrounds organs and behaves differently, metabolically, from fat stored elsewhere — though a circumference-based shape result can't confirm how much visceral fat someone actually has; it's a correlated pattern, not a direct measurement. In contrast, fat stored in the hips and thighs — characteristic of the pear shape — is generally associated with lower cardiovascular risk at a comparable body size, tracking with lower WHR values in population-level data. This is a general pattern, not an individual guarantee; lifestyle, genetics and many other factors matter too. Research has also linked WHR to fertility: women with a WHR above 0.80 have been found to have significantly lower pregnancy rates than those between 0.70 and 0.79, independent of BMI, while men around 0.90 tend to show better fertility markers than those notably higher or lower — suggesting fat distribution carries hormonal dimensions beyond cardiovascular health. See the full breakdown on our <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist-to-hip ratio calculator</a>.</p>

    <h3>WHR and WHtR vs. BMI — Which Is a Better Health Indicator?</h3>
    <p>BMI (Body Mass Index) — weight (kg) ÷ height² (m²) — has been the dominant health screening tool for decades, but it has real limitations. It cannot distinguish fat mass from muscle mass, so a highly muscular athlete can register as "obese." It tells you nothing about where fat is distributed — the question WHR was specifically designed to answer. And its formula was developed by the statistician Adolphe Quetelet in the 1830s using data drawn almost entirely from white European men; later research that popularized it as "BMI" (Ancel Keys, 1972) also drew mainly on European, American and Japanese men — a narrow base that has been shown to misclassify risk in people of Asian, South Asian and African descent. The 2015 Annals of Internal Medicine study cited above is a clear example: "normal-weight central obesity," invisible to BMI, carried a higher mortality risk than obesity classified by BMI alone. The practical takeaway: use BMI as a rough starting point if needed, but use WHR and WHtR for a fuller picture of where health risk actually sits. None of these metrics replace a full clinical assessment.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="btc-sec" id="btc-sizeshape">
    <h2>Body Shape vs. Body Size — Why They Are Not the Same Thing <span class="btc-unique">Often overlooked</span></h2>
    <p><strong>Body shape</strong> refers to the proportional relationship between your measurements — your silhouette, whether you are wider at the hips, broader at the shoulders, or balanced on both sides of the waist. Shape is about differences, not absolute numbers. <strong>Body size</strong> refers to the actual numerical values — your weight, height, dress size or BMI. Two people who weigh exactly the same and share the same height can have completely different body shapes; two people with the same shape — say, both pear-shaped — might wear completely different clothing sizes.</p>
    <p>This matters practically: clothing advice based on body shape is size-agnostic — the styling tips that work for a pear shape apply whether that person is a size 8 or a size 22. Understanding your shape independent of your size also helps decouple appearance from weight, which is a useful step toward a healthier relationship with your body.</p>
    <div class="btc-note">
      <p>Your body shape can remain the same across different sizes. Many people find that even after significant weight gain or loss, their proportional shape classification stays consistent — they simply become a larger or smaller version of the same shape.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="btc-sec" id="btc-changes">
    <h2>How Your Body Shape Can Change Over Time <span class="btc-unique">Often overlooked</span></h2>
    <p>Your body shape is not fixed for life. While genetics establish a strong baseline, several major life factors can meaningfully shift your proportions over the years — sometimes gradually, sometimes quite significantly.</p>

    <h3>Age-Related Changes</h3>
    <p>As estrogen levels decline with age, women often experience a redistribution of fat from the hips and thighs toward the abdomen. A person who had a pear or hourglass shape in their twenties and thirties may find their waist measurement growing proportionally faster than their hips in their forties and fifties — sometimes shifting their classification toward rectangle or even apple. This is entirely normal and does not represent a failure of any kind.</p>

    <h3>Weight Gain and Weight Loss</h3>
    <p>Weight changes affect body shape differently depending on where your body preferentially stores or loses fat, which is largely genetic. Some people gain primarily in the abdomen (shifting toward apple), others in the hips (staying pear). Weight loss may not change your shape proportionally either — you might simply become a smaller version of your existing shape. The calculator is most useful when taken at a stable weight. To preview a change before you commit to it, use Compare mode in our <a href="/body-visualizer/">body visualizer</a>: set your current measurements as body A, change body B, and lay one over the other.</p>

    <h3>Pregnancy and Postpartum Changes</h3>
    <p>Pregnancy temporarily, and sometimes permanently, alters body shape — the ribcage can expand, hip bones may widen slightly, and fat distribution often shifts postpartum. Many women find their classification changes after pregnancy, commonly from hourglass or pear toward rectangle or apple, due to abdominal muscle changes and fat redistribution. Postpartum measurements can keep changing for months, so results may be more useful once your body has settled into a stable pattern.</p>

    <h3>Exercise and Muscle Development</h3>
    <p>Strength training can meaningfully change your measurements and therefore your shape classification. Building your shoulders and upper back can shift a pear toward a rectangle or even top hourglass; developing glutes can shift a rectangle toward bottom hourglass or pear. This is one reason body shape and fitness goals are deeply connected — targeted training can sculpt your proportions over time.</p>
    <p><strong>When to recalculate:</strong> if your weight has changed by more than 10–15 pounds, you have gone through pregnancy or a major hormonal shift, or you have been strength training consistently for 6+ months, it's worth retaking your measurements.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="btc-sec" id="btc-men">
    <h2>Body Shape for Men</h2>
    <p>This calculator is built around female proportions — bust, waist, high hip and hips. Men have their own measurement-based system with five shapes — trapezoid, inverted triangle, rectangle, triangle and oval — built around chest, waist and hips instead, covered in full depth (with styling guidance for each) on our dedicated <a href="/male-body-type-calculator/">body type calculator for men</a>. You can also see all five male shapes in 3D in our <a href="/body-visualizer/">male body visualizer</a>. Separately, fitness culture often discusses male build through a different lens entirely: somatotype.</p>

    <h3>Ectomorph, Mesomorph and Endomorph</h3>
    <p>Somatotype is a broader classification than measurement-based shape — it describes how a build tends to respond to training and diet rather than current proportions. <strong>Ectomorph:</strong> naturally lean with a fast metabolism and narrow shoulders, often struggling to gain weight or muscle despite eating well — usually falls into the rectangle shape category. <strong>Mesomorph:</strong> the stereotypically athletic build, naturally broad-shouldered with muscle that builds easily — usually measures as inverted triangle or trapezoid. <strong>Endomorph:</strong> a larger frame with higher body fat and a wider waist, gaining weight more easily — usually measures as oval or apple. Most people are a mix of two somatotypes rather than a pure example of one; the terms are reference points, not boxes.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="btc-sec" id="btc-faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>What measurements do I need to use this calculator?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>You need three measurements: bust or chest, waist, and hip, all in the same unit — either inches or centimetres. High hip is optional and adds a bonus ratio to your result. Age, height and weight are optional extras that unlock a BMI reading and an age-context note.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>Is this body type calculator for men too?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>This calculator is built around female proportions, so it's the "body type calculator female" version if that's the phrase you searched. Men have their own five-shape system based on chest, waist and hips instead — the men's calculator is linked in the navigation above.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>How accurate is a body type calculator?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>It's accurate in the sense that it correctly applies the classification formula to your measurements. The bigger variable is measurement accuracy — measure twice, use a fabric tape (not a rigid ruler), wear minimal clothing, keep your posture natural, and don't round numbers significantly.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>Why do different calculators give different body shape results?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>Calculators use different systems — four-shape versus seven-shape — some include the high hip measurement and some don't, and small rounding differences can push a borderline result from one shape to another. If two tools disagree, you're probably on the border between those two shapes, meaning the styling tips for both apply to you.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>What if I fall between two body types?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>Very common and completely normal — the boundaries between shapes are gradients, not sharp lines. In practice that means more options, not fewer: try the tips for both shapes and keep whichever matches how your clothes actually fit.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>Can your body shape change over time or with exercise?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>Yes, and quite significantly. Pregnancy, weight changes, ageing and hormonal shifts all change your proportions, and targeted strength training changes measurements around the shoulders, back, waist and hips. See the section above for the full breakdown by cause. Re-measure every six months rather than treating one result as permanent.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>What is the most common female body shape?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>The rectangle (also called banana or straight) is the most commonly cited female body shape, with figures from a 2006 NC State analysis putting it around 46% of women. Pear and apple are next most cited, while the classic hourglass — often considered the "ideal" — is actually the least common in that same data, at around 8%.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>What is the difference between hourglass and pear body shape?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>An hourglass has a bust and hip that are nearly equal in size, with a dramatically smaller waist in between. A pear shape has hips noticeably wider than the bust, with the waist moderately defined but not dramatically so. In simple terms: hourglass is about symmetry and a defined waist; pear is about the lower body being wider than the upper.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>What is high hip measurement and why is it included?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>The high hip is measured approximately 3 to 4 inches (8–10 cm) below your natural waist, at the upper part of your hip bone — above the fullest part of your buttocks. It doesn't change your shape category on its own, but it feeds the High Hip–Waist ratio shown in your result, which helps flag a pronounced "shelf" pattern that a plain bust/waist/hip reading can miss.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>Is hourglass really the "ideal" body shape?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>Historically, Western fashion has treated it that way, and research does suggest a waist-to-hip ratio near 0.7 is widely perceived as attractive across many cultures. But "widely perceived" and "universally ideal" are different things, and beauty standards vary by culture, era and personal taste. The hourglass is one of seven shapes, not the benchmark for the rest.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>Does body shape depend on weight or size?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>No. Body shape is determined by proportions — the relationships between your measurements — not by weight or dress size. A slim person can have an apple-shaped body and a larger person can have an hourglass figure. See the "Body Shape vs. Body Size" section above for more on why these are separate conversations.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>Is there a body shape calculator that just uses height and weight?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>Not for the hourglass/pear/apple system — that specifically needs bust, waist and hip measurements, since it's about proportion rather than size. Height and weight alone can give you BMI, and with a wrist measurement added, a body frame size — our <a href="/body-frame-size-calculator/">body frame size calculator</a> covers that version directly.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>Does this calculator use shoulder measurements?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>No — this calculator uses bust, waist, high hip and hip only. Shoulder width is a real factor in how broad-shouldered shapes like inverted triangle actually look, but it isn't collected here; bust width serves as the practical stand-in for upper-body breadth in this measurement system.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>Is spoon a body shape this calculator can give me?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>No — this calculator's seven shapes are hourglass, top hourglass, bottom hourglass, pear, apple, inverted triangle and rectangle. "Spoon" is a related term some other systems use for a pear shape with a pronounced high-hip shelf; here, that pattern is captured within the pear result rather than split into its own category.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>How often should I re-measure?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>Every six months is enough for most people, or after any significant change — pregnancy, a notable weight shift, a new training programme, or a hormonal transition such as perimenopause. Measuring more often mostly captures normal daily fluctuation rather than a real change in proportion.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>What's the difference between body type and body shape?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>In everyday use the terms are often interchangeable, and this site uses them that way. More strictly, body shape refers to visible proportions from your bust, waist and hip measurements, while body type can also describe frame, build, or somatotype in other contexts such as fitness (see the ectomorph/mesomorph/endomorph section above).</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>What body type do I have if my waist is bigger than my hips?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>That pattern usually points toward apple, especially if your bust and hips are close in size. The exact result still depends on how your bust compares to both waist and hips, so it's worth running your full numbers through the calculator above rather than judging from the waist alone.</p>
    </div></details>

    <details class="btc-faq"><summary>Does body type affect clothing size?</summary><div class="btc-faq-body">
      <p>Yes. Two people can wear the same size label and still need very different cuts, because size measures overall volume while body type describes where that volume sits. That's why the same dress size can fit differently on an hourglass versus a rectangle — the number on the tag doesn't capture proportion.</p>
    </div></details>
  </section>

  <p class="btc-disc">This calculator is an educational and styling tool. It does not diagnose any medical condition and its results are not a substitute for advice from a qualified healthcare professional. If you have concerns about your weight, waist-to-hip ratio or general health, speak to a doctor.</p>

  </div>
